h a grant from  amgen in 2003  and we're focused on instruction and  research in  all kinds of biopharmaceutical  processing including mammalian and  microbial  cell culture and fermentation stem cell  research  bioseparations and purification and  we're building a stronger and stronger  set of tools in data analytics  so overall biopharmaceutical processing  and where it is today  we really are in a place where things  are changing  significantly so there is this  concept of three pillars of medicine you  can see the the graphic here  and you know we started with molecular  therapies 150 years ago  and have grown through monoclonal  antibodies largest  molecules in that space into gene  therapies  and cellular therapies where we're  trying to either bring  a gene and correct that genetic material  so we achieve the therapeutic benefit or  where we're actually trying to change  cells whether in vivo or in vitro to  affect an improved therapeutic outcome  so i want to shift then to think about  what do we  need as talent to support that broad set  of modalities that's coming down the  pipeline  so when we talk to executives around the  world  um and ask what are the biggest  challenges the  constraints and talent are a large piece  of that uh  challenge for the future and you can see  that um  when hiring managers talk about who  they're looking for  they're looking for positions outside of  r d  that translation space how do we bring  this product  from the lab and into the therapeutic  area so the most challenging  areas to fill are in this bioprocessing  space  so regulatory affairs process  development quality  and you can see this comes from a over  arching survey that was done by the  coalition of state biosciences institute  and  in addition um we have a constraint not  just on  what our our talent is capable of  but how much talent we have so right now  we're about  to head down into a constraint on talent  just driven  out of demographics so you can see here  that what's shown is the population of  18 year olds  in any given year and you can see that  as of 2021 we'll be at the lowest point  in a long time for the number of 18 year  olds which of course is the feed  into our undergraduate universities and  they feed into our  stem talent pool it does grow over the  course of the next few years but then it  looks to plummet radically so  when thinking about how to address the  problems of talent it's not just a  question of what they do  but how many we have and how efficient  are we in addition um  until recently we had very low  unemployment  and obviously the impact of the  coronavirus  on our overall economy is still yet to  be defined  but there is still  a large competition for the limited  amount of talent we have  and hopefully once our countries are all  back  up and running again we will see again  that  opportunity for full employment which  therefore often reduces the drivers for  higher education so  oftentimes we see in these times of high  employment  that students don't pursue those higher  level degrees that are there and are  required for the scientific and  technical  aspects of our industry and then i  talked a little bit about how our  industry is changing  and how the technical requirements  around the industry are changing so  not only are there more and more  technology platforms related to the  different  types of products but there's more  technology for how we make each product  and there's more need to cross those  platforms with each other and that  multi-disciplinary focus is getting  more and more important  so what's it look like going forward  into the future  so when asked the biggest challenge for  the growth of the industry you can see  there's a number of items that are  listed but the second  item listed by executives around the  world  is the staff and technically trained  staff so  where are we going to get that  technically trained staff it's changing  then who it is  and who that staff is so the  workforce is going to be more and more  diverse and you can see  um these are a couple of graphics from a  great report from deloitte on the  biopharma workforce  and you can see that we'll be looking  for  people with different sets of skills as  well as different  kinds of people and people will be  contributing to our industry  from different avenues so we won't be  having  everybody be a full-time employee who  shows up to the manufacturing side every  day we have to learn how  to reach out and work with employees who  are around the globe  in different places at different times  and contributing what their skills  can bring to our new digital  environments  so that's a little bit about the talent  constraints and and where we might be  headed  so where's the industry going so what  you hear in a lot of  conversations is talking about pharma  4.0  so in introducing pharma 4.0 it's good  to start with  what's this concept of 4.0 so what is  manufacturing 4.0 so  you can see the concept comes out of a  uh  it was actually a german article i think  that coined the terms that the the first  generation of industrial resolute  revolutions was  really um water and steam power when  when machines were able to be operated  beyond human power and then the second  industrial revolution  is aligned to mass production the  assembly line  and the wide availability of electricity  and kind of the early um  to 1900s and then somewhere in the 1970s  1980s computers and automation became  more and more important  for all of our industries and we started  to be able to  tell our machines and equipment what to  do through computers  this fourth generation which is what we  talk about when we're focusing on  manufacturing 4.0  is the cyber physical systems talking to  each other  so rather than humans driving through  automated computer systems  the automation will talk to other  automation and  gather information in order to drive  things forward  so in discussing that there's a few  points that are important  one is the the idea of cyber physical  systems or cps  so overarching the 4.0 is really the  delivery of this concept where your  physical plant your supply chain your  shipment  your raw materials the handling all gets  mapped into a virtual environment and  therefore can be manipulated and  operated on by  other virtual systems and it's the full  digitalization  that gives us this opportunity for 4.0  manufacturing  in addition you have the integration of  the internet of things  so we're integrating technology and  sensors  that are speaking wirelessly across  different pieces of equipment  and systems and that allows us using  analytical tools to make real-time  decisions and that's  really the benefit is that we have a  constant feedback loop inside  our overall manufacturing process so to  ensure that we're constantly delivering  the most efficient that we can  and then finally the digital  transformation  impacts everything that we do it's not a  digital transformation  that is talking just about your  manufacturing environment  or robots operating your packaging line  it's talking about  being able to manage your data through  your supply chain and through your  shipment channels as well  so from raw materials to customers a way  to think of it  so here's a great depiction from uh  tony mary's toulouse at astrazeneca  um thinking about how does this impact  us in the pharmaceutical industry  so how we work today we really have  um a lot of different steps that are  still  very manual even in our most  in our most automated facilities and  you can see starting at number one that  we have  paper batch records in a lot of places  still we're getting  more and more to electronic batch  records but we still have a lot of  paper log books calibration records um  all kinds of things that are paper and  because they're paper we don't have  real-time data so we can't make  real-time decisions  and in addition we still have a lot of  manual  and repetitive work and that limits how  efficient we can be  and anytime we continue to rely on  manual steps  we of course increase human error  anytime we have humans we have error  so what we're looking for um is going to  move us out of that space  in addition the supply chain as i  mentioned is the piece of this the  supply  chain visibility is often manual and  we're still relying  on often very  highly evolved spreadsheets but still  spreadsheets  and then we have paper-based  instructions for how to move things  between  and then the lack of visibility in the  movement of our goods we don't know  where things are in the supply chain  where we want to get to in a digitalized  environment  would be to have access to these data so  if we take everything that we just  discussed  and we digitize it we would go from  paper batch records to electronic batch  records that  are not only recording but are  predicting  and enabling us to use analytics as  feedback control to make sure we  constantly stay in the most optimal  space for the best quality product  we would therefore have real-time data  for continuing to manage that process  control as i mentioned  um more and more we could rely  on automated and robotic solutions to  move  physical things forward through our  processes  have the ideal environment where we can  visualize  our end-to-end supply chain and we know  where our product is  at any given time and then looking at  other  wireless solutions um wearable and mo  mobile  type solutions that give us data  without having to think about  translating that data so  imagine somebody is writing with a  a wireless pen we automatically capture  that data  rather than having to transcribe it back  into a computer system later or wearing  a pair of goggles that just  looks at a balance and sees the weights  and records that way directly into our  electronic batch record going forward  so all of this iot and digitalization  brings data real time  to our systems and this is what we call  farmer 4.0  so the impact on our talent pool is  going to be significant  and talking through a little bit of that  um  there's going to be a obviously we've  heard a lot in this  presentation already about that  interconnection of the real and virtual  world  and our people our employees our staff  are going to have to understand  what that means and how they fit into  that part they're always going to be in  the real world  but this virtual world is going to be  existing and operating around them  there's going to be decentralized  intelligence that means it's not all  decisions are getting made on the floor  or by a supervisor or a manager  and so we're going to have all these  object-related  decision making going on and you need to  understand the impact of that the risk  of that and make sure that our systems  are built  to for success in the long term  um autom autonomous process management  is obviously a goal it's something  honestly that's been talked about  since i was in this industry since i  first joined over 30 years ago we've  been talking about  lights out facilities and the  opportunity for our  facilities to run without staff there to  move things forward  and then data management um a  tool that we need to think about is what  do we do with data we get  lots and lots of data but it's not  helpful unless we can move it through an  analysis and take action and only after  we've taken action have we  brought value to the data so thinking  about all of this  and what it means for our talent um  based on a  a report that was  built um again by deloitte and was  presented  uh recently at davos here's some quotes  that i think are  really meaningful there aren't too many  people we can take straight out of  university who have everything we need  so as academics we need to take this  feedback and say  what do we need to be doing because  we're adding  a burden to our customers if they have  to train people  after they leave the academic  environment  the it's hard for business to ask  governments to prepare the students and  i read that as government  and academia because things are changing  so rapidly and the speed by which the  education  system changed is a completely different  track and really the point there  is that academics aren't paying enough  attention to how fast we need to move to  keep up  with our partners and and our customers  and industry  and last the  fact that the focus is on needing more  group-based and project-based activities  because this is how we work  and this is how we'll work more and more  and  i would overlay on top of that that  these need to be virtual group based and  project-based activities  and all of us with our response to  coronavirus are now  seeing how we can work virtually and how  we can still maintain our group  activities and push our project forward  and we need to take ownership of that  and move that forward in the  academic world so another thing to think  about  is a what are the skills that are  required  so we tend to think about skill  requirements and we think about  technical skills i need to teach  somebody how to operate a line i need to  teach somebody what a  cell culture should look like in reality  when we  survey industry participants we find  that  more and more the focus is on softer  skills and on the ability to  step up and deliver new solutions so of  course complex problem solving which  incorporates all those  technical skills stays at the top but  what we see  is that what's increased over the last  five years is critical thinking  and creativity that we really need  people to be evaluating that data  and if you think about this how this  relates to digital organization  that data we need to know that it makes  sense so we need people  thinking critically about every step and  the inputs and then  being creative about how to bring that  together and how to drive towards  solution  and a couple things that are new on the  list for 2020  emotional intelligence because we're  going to be working across so many  different sets of skills  and we need teams to deliver and not  individuals  this becomes more and more critical as  we move forward and harder and harder to  deliver honestly across virtual  platforms  and so we need to focus on building  those skills in that virtual space  and then cognitive flexibility and this  is about  being able to learn new skills this is  meeting that business demand for a  changing environment  and teaching our students not what they  know but how to learn  more how to learn going forward and so  we're not trying to only focus on  content we need to teach students to  teach themselves  how do they get on google and solve a  problem rather than  having a professor teach them so  those are a couple of skills and in the  trade-offs um for those skills  what decreased and fell out of the list  coordinating with others and negotiation  which i think are built into some of  these other activities so they slipped  down a little bit  but they're still important but what  left was uh  quality control and active listening um  but i i would argue that uh  the critical thinking that coordinates  and covers quality control and  emotional intelligence clearly requires  active thinking so i think it's a  broadening of that concept rather than  an elimination  so what does this mean in  detail for our skills that we need for  biopharmaceutical manufacturing how do  we bring that together  so the skills for the pharma 4.0 that we  talked about  have to encompass all the emerging  technologies like continuous  manufacturing salvaging therapy  process analytical technologies in order  to get that ongoing data acquisition  in addition to some of the things that  we've talked about  for years and process understanding  we've been talking about quality by  design and continuous process  verification we need more  understanding and a more  multi-disciplinary  commitment to understanding compliance  and regulatory affairs  and that is going to happen through a  better understanding of data integrity  we need rapid release particularly to  support some of these new modalities  like cell and gene therapy there just  isn't time for 60 day  release turn around windows and  automated  file updates as well so electronic ctd  give us the opportunity  to constantly update our files so that  the agencies understand what we're doing  and make sure that we're not putting  patients at risk around the world  as well as the hopeful opportunity to be  able to roll  out an important change around the world  much more rapidly than we can do now it  can take up to two years to make a  change in our regulatory filing systems  which means we can't get inventory at  the highest level  of quality with our most recent  processes for two years to some of our  patients  and then last we have to bring in a  stronger understanding of data  management  really that inability as i talked about  to go from acquisition and aggregation  to  analysis and action so that we bring  value out of our data  and that data management has to  encompass broader tools  we have to be able to implement the  internet of things the wireless  sensing and as it covers inventory the  instruments on our equipment  the robotics we need that information  constantly flowing  and constantly being transformed into  meaningful information for action  and coupled with that is going to be  artificial intelligence  as well as machine learning and  real-time feedback  so you can see all of these things and  these are the technical skills and how  they  they deliver for pharma 4.0 and we think  about that when we think about  building the capabilities of our new  talent  a little bit more about um the  employment requirements so  beyond the technical skills as i  mentioned there's a  focus on the soft skills and  the drive for multi-disciplinary  knowledge is just  getting stronger and stronger because we  have to be able to communicate with each  other  it doesn't mean that somebody who is a  scientist  needs to therefore understand the  details of software  development for robotics but it does  need to be  that they're aware enough of those  robotics and their uses that they can  explain  what they can get out of it and what  their requirements are from a scientific  point of view  so it's building those engagement and  that multi-disciplinary conversation  that's going to drive the most  successful companies  we need to integrate quality and  regulatory into our operations they  can't be  a secondary thing they need to be a part  of the operations  and therefore all our employees  staff as well have to be able to  understand their quality and regulatory  environments  and then business and financial  operations we're making decisions on the  floor  every day that have business and  financial impact and  if we don't train our staff to  understand those  we will be as successful as we could be  what pharma 4.0 brings to us is the  opportunity for higher level operations  and oversight  but what it means in terms of  implementation  we will be starting with task-based  automation so we automate  certain tasks and then we build that  together to get to that higher level  oversight  and part of that has to be the data  interpretation and response  and understanding how to bring those  models  to give us the the input and the  process controls that we're looking for  rapid reskilling as i mentioned that our  talent  our skills have to be developed over and  over and over again  there's been major shifts every 10 years  in this  environment and i think they're getting  faster so  you can imagine 10 years ago we didn't  have discussions around crispr gene  therapy car t mrna the  microbiome was just coming out as a  a treatment platform it's amazing how  fast things change in a decade  and yet we come into this field and we  contribute 