  that what's shown is the population of  18 year olds  in any given year and you can see that  as of 2021 we'll be at the lowest point  in a long time for the number of 18 year  olds which of course is the feed  into our undergraduate universities and  they feed into our  stem talent pool it does grow over the  course of the next few years but then it  looks to plummet radically so  when thinking about how to address the  problems of talent it's not just a  question of what they do  but how many we have and how efficient  are we in addition um  until recently we had very low  unemployment  and obviously the impact of the  coronavirus  on our overall economy is still yet to  be defined  but there is still  a large competition for the limited  amount of talent we have  and hopefully once our countries are all  back  up and running again we will see again  that  opportunity for full employment which  therefore often reduces the drivers for  higher education so  oftentimes we see in these times of high  employment  that students don't pursue those higher  level degrees that are there and are  required for the scientific and  technical  aspects of our industry and then i  talked a little bit about how our  industry is changing  and how the technical requirements  around the industry are changing so  not only are there more and more  technology platforms related to the  different  types of products but there's more  technology for how we make each product  and there's more need to cross those  platforms with each other and that  multi-disciplinary focus is getting  more and more important  so what's it look like going forward  into the future  so when asked the biggest challenge for  the growth of the industry you can see  there's a number of items that are  listed but the second  item listed by executives around the  world  is the staff and technically trained  staff so  where are we going to get that  technically trained staff it's changing  then who it is  and who that staff is so the  workforce is going to be more and more  diverse and you can see  um these are a couple of graphics from a  great report from deloitte on the  biopharma workforce  and you can see that we'll be looking  for  people with different sets of skills as  well as different  kinds of people and people will be  contributing to our industry  from different avenues so we won't be  having  everybody be a full-time employee who  shows up to the manufacturing side every  day we have to learn how  to reach out and work with employees who  are around the globe  in different places at different times  and contributing what their skills  can bring to our new digital  environments  so that's a little bit about the talent  constraints and and where we might be  headed  so where's the industry going so what  you hear in a lot of  conversations is talking about pharma  4.0  so in introducing pharma 4.0 it's good  to start with  what's this concept of 4.0 so what is  manufacturing 4.0 so  you can see the concept comes out of a  uh  it was actually a german article i think  that coined the terms that the the first  generation of industrial resolute  revolutions was  really um water and steam power when  when machines were able to be operated  beyond human power and then the second  industrial revolution  is aligned to mass production the  assembly line  and the wide availability of electricity  and kind of the early um  to 1900s and then somewhere in the 1970s  1980s computers and automation became  more and more important  for all of our industries and we started  to be able to  tell our machines and equipment what to  do through computers  this fourth generation which is what we  talk about when we're focusing on  manufacturing 4.0  is the cyber physical systems talking to  each other  so rather than humans driving through  automated computer systems  the automation will talk to other  automation and  gather information in order to drive  things forward  so in discussing that there's a few  points that are important  one is the the idea of cyber physical  systems or cps  so overarching the 4.0 is really the  delivery of this concept where your  physical plant your supply chain your  shipment  your raw materials the handling all gets  mapped into a virtual environment and  therefore can be manipulated and  operated on by  other virtual systems and it's the full  digitalization  that gives us this opportunity for 4.0  manufacturing  in addition you have the integration of  the internet of things  so we're integrating technology and  sensors  that are speaking wirelessly across  different pieces of equipment  and systems and that allows us using  analytical tools to make real-time  decisions and that's  really the benefit is that we have a  constant feedback loop inside  our overall manufacturing process so to  ensure that we're constantly delivering  the most efficient that we can  and then finally the digital  transformation  impacts everything that we do it's not a  digital transformation  that is talking just about your  manufacturing environment  or robots operating your packaging line  it's talking about  being able to manage your data through  your supply chain and through your  shipment channels as well  so from raw materials to customers a way  to think of it  so here's a great depiction from uh  tony mary's toulouse at astrazeneca  um thinking about how does this impact  us in the pharmaceutical industry  so how we work today we really have  um a lot of different steps that are  still  very manual even in our most  in our most automated facilities and  you can see starting at number one that  we have  paper batch records in a lot of places  still we're getting  more and more to electronic batch  records but we still have a lot of  paper log books calibration records um  all kinds of things that are paper and  because they're paper we don't have  real-time data so we can't make  real-time decisions  and in addition we still have a lot of  manual  and repetitive work and that limits how  efficient we can be  and anytime we continue to rely on  manual steps  we of course increase human error  anytime we have humans we have error  so what we're looking for um is going to  move us out of that space  in addition the supply chain as i  mentioned is the piece of this the  supply  chain visibility is often manual and  we're still relying  on often very  highly evolved spreadsheets but still  spreadsheets  and then we have paper-based  instructions for how to move things  between  and then the lack of visibility in the  movement of our goods we don't know  where things are in the supply chain  where we want to get to in a digitalized  environment  would be to have access to these data so  if we take everything that we just  discussed  and we digitize it we would go from  paper batch records to electronic batch  records that  are not only recording but are  predicting  and enabling us to use analytics as  feedback control to make sure we  constantly stay in the most optimal  space for the best quality product  we would therefore have real-time data  for continuing to manage that process  control as i mentioned  um more and more we could rely  on automated and robotic solutions to  move  physical things forward through our  processes  have the ideal environment where we can  visualize  our end-to-end supply chain and we know  where our product is  at any given time and then looking at  other  wireless solutions um wearable and mo  mobile  type solutions that give us data  without having to think about  translating that data so  imagine somebody is writing with a  a wireless pen we automatically capture  that data  rather than having to transcribe it back  into a computer system later or wearing  a pair of goggles that just  looks at a balance and sees the weights  and records that way directly into our  electronic batch record going forward  so all of this iot and digitalization  brings data real time  to our systems and this is what we call  farmer 4.0  so the impact on our talent pool is  going to be significant  and talking through a little bit of that  um  there's going to be a obviously we've  heard a lot in this  presentation already about that  interconnection of the real and virtual  world  and our people our employees our staff  are going to have to understand  what that means and how they fit into  that part they're always going to be in  the real world  but this virtual world is going to be  existing and operating around them  there's going to be decentralized  intelligence that means it's not all  decisions are getting made on the floor  or by a supervisor or a manager  and so we're going to have all these  object-related  decision making going on and you need to  understand the impact of that the risk  of that and make sure that our systems  are built  to for success in the long term  um autom autonomous process management  is obviously a goal it's something  honestly that's been talked about  since i was in this industry since i  first joined over 30 years ago we've  been talking about  lights out facilities and the  opportunity for our  facilities to run without staff there to  move things forward  and then data management um a  tool that we need to think about is what  do we do with data we get  lots and lots of data but it's not  helpful unless we can move it through an  analysis and take action and only after  we've taken action have we  brought value to the data so thinking  about all of this  and what it means for our talent um  based on a  a report that was  built um again by deloitte and was  presented  uh recently at davos here's some quotes  that i think are  really meaningful there aren't too many  people we can take straight out of  university who have everything we need  so as academics we need to take this  feedback and say  what do we need to be doing because  we're adding  a burden to our customers if they have  to train people  after they leave the academic  environment  the it's hard for business to ask  governments to prepare the students and  i read that as government  and academia because things are changing  so rapidly and the speed by which the  education  system changed is a completely different  track and really the point there  is that academics aren't paying enough  attention to how fast we need to move to  keep up  with our partners and and our customers  and industry  and last the  fact that the focus is on needing more  group-based and project-based activities  because this is how we work  and this is how we'll work more and more  and  i would overlay on top of that that  these need to be virtual group based and  project-based activities  and all of us with our response to  coronavirus are now  seeing how we can work virtually and how  we can still maintain our group  activities and push our project forward  and we need to take ownership of that  and move that forward in the  academic world so another thing to think  about  is a what are the skills that are  required  so we tend to think about skill  requirements and we think about  technical skills i need to teach  somebody how to operate a line i need to  teach somebody what a  cell culture should look like in reality  when we  survey industry participants we find  that  more and more the focus is on softer  skills and on the ability to  step up and deliver new solutions so of  course complex problem solving which  incorporates all those  technical skills stays at the top but  what we see  is that what's increased over the last  five years is critical thinking  and creativity that we really need  people to be evaluating that data  and if you think about this how this  relates to digital organization  that data we need to know that it makes  sense so we need people  thinking critically about every step and  the inputs and then  being creative about how to bring that  together and how to drive towards  solution  and a couple things that are new on the  list for 2020  emotional intelligence because we're  going to be working across so many  different sets of skills  and we need teams to deliver and not  individuals  this becomes more and more critical as  we move forward and harder and harder to  deliver honestly across virtual  platforms  and so we need to focus on building  those skills in that virtual space  and then cognitive flexibility and this  is about  being able to learn new skills this is  meeting that business demand for a  changing environment  and teaching our students not what they  know but how to learn  more how to learn going forward and so  we're not trying to only focus on  content we need to teach students to  teach themselves  how do they get on google and solve a  problem rather than  having a professor teach them so  those are a couple of skills and in the  trade-offs um for those skills  what decreased and fell out of the list  coordinating with others and negotiation  which i think are built into some of  these other activities so they slipped  down a little bit  but they're still important but what  left was uh  quality control and active listening um  but i i would argue that uh  the critical thinking that coordinates  and covers quality control and  emotional intelligence clearly requires  active thinking so i think it's a  broadening of that concept rather than  an elimination  so what does this mean in  detail for our skills that we need for  biopharmaceutical manufacturing how do  we bring that together  so the skills for the pharma 4.0 that we  talked about  have to encompass all the emerging  technologies like continuous  manufacturing salvaging therapy  process analytical technologies in order  to get that ongoing data acquisition  in addition to some of the things that  we've talked about  for years and process understanding  we've been talking about quality by  design and continuous process  verification we need more  understanding and a more  multi-disciplinary  commitment to understanding compliance  and regulatory affairs  and that is going to happen through a  better understanding of data integrity  we need rapid release particularly to  support some of these new modalities  like cell and gene therapy there just  isn't time for 60 day  release turn around windows and  automated  file updates as well so electronic ctd  give us the opportunity  to constantly update our files so that  the agencies understand what we're doing  and make sure that we're not putting  patients at risk around the world  as well as the hopeful opportunity to be  able to roll  out an important change around the world  much more rapidly than we can do now it  can take up to two years to make a  change in our regulatory filing systems  which means we can't get inventory at  the highest level  of quality with our most recent  processes for two years to some of our  patients  and then last we have to bring in a  stronger understanding of data  management  really that inability as i talked about  to go from acquisition and aggregation  to  analysis and action so that we bring  value out of our data  and that data management has to  encompass broader tools  we have to be able to implement the  internet of things the wireless  sensing and as it covers inventory the  instruments on our equipment  the robotics we need that information  constantly flowing  and constantly being transformed into  meaningful information for action  and coupled with that is going to be  artificial intelligence  as well as machine learning and  real-time feedback  so you can see all of these things and  these are the technical skills and how  they  they deliver for pharma 4.0 and we think  about that when we think about  building the capabilities of our new  talent  a little bit more about um the  employment requirements so  beyond the technical skills as i  mentioned there's a  focus on the soft skills and  the drive for multi-disciplinary  knowledge is just  getting stronger and stronger because we  have to be able to communicate with each  other  it doesn't mean that somebody who is a  scientist  needs to therefore understand the  details of software  development for robotics but it does  need to be  that they're aware enough of those  robotics and their uses that they can  explain  what they can get out of it and what  their requirements are from a scientific  point of view  so it's building those engagement and  that multi-disciplinary conversation  that's going to drive the most  successful companies  we need to integrate quality and  regulatory into our operations they  can't be  a secondary thing they need to be a part  of the operations  and therefore all our employees  staff as well have to be able to  understand their quality and regulatory  environments  and then business and financial  operations we're making decisions on the  floor  every day that have business and  financial impact and  if we don't train our staff to  understand those  we will be as successful as we could be  what pharma 4.0 brings to us is the  opportunity for higher level operations  and oversight  but what it means in terms of  implementation  we will be starting with task-based  automation so we automate  certain tasks and then we build that  together to get to that higher level  oversight  and part of that has to be the data  interpretation and response  and understanding how to bring those  models  to give us the the input and the  process controls that we're looking for  rapid reskilling as i mentioned that our  talent  our skills have to be developed over and  over and over again  there's been major shifts every 10 years  in this  environment and i think they're getting  faster so  you can imagine 10 years ago we didn't  have discussions around crispr gene  therapy car t mrna the  microbiome was just coming out as a  a treatment platform it's amazing how  fast things change in a decade  and yet we come into this field and we  contribute four to five decades which  means we have to be relearning  four to five times at least and maybe  more  as things tool faster and faster and  then  the critical and soft skills that we  really need to focus on  um innovation and teamwork and managing  our resources to make sure  everybody is efficiently delivering  towards the value of  our organization so  if those are all the requirements and  these are the pressures on our industry  as we get towards 4.0  what are our opportunities for talent  development  and as an academic institution we're  looking  out into our business world and to our  partners to better understand  what do we need to be delivering for  talent capabilities  so one of the considerations um  for employee development there's a broad  set of things to think about when you  when you think about how to develop an  employee to be  uh aligned to the value they need to  bring to your business  and this is a model developed by  um the uh the labor and statistics  board of united states and you can see  what it does is bucket things into two  dimensions  so we have the horizontal dimension that  looks at things that are occupational  specific on the right those skill sets  that  apply to your given occupation and then  things that are important across  occupations  um you can see to the left and then you  have the vertical  where we have things that are at the top  focused on the worker  as an individual and things at the  bottom that are focused on the job  and so in the middle is obviously what  we deliver as a full employee  so where we can deliver as an academic  institution  is really looking at worker requirements  and experience requirements and this is  the space  that we're really trying to focus on  understanding  and looking for collaboration with  industry  to build so if you think about  educational opportunities in a kind of  uh pyramid shape as a lot of things  are you can say that the top of the  pyramid  is the elite research and undergraduate  universities  and you can quickly come up with a list  of these universities these are the ones  you think of and you hear about  all the time on the news and in research  platforms and  there's a secondary level that's also  very active all of these provide  very strong training opportunities and  excellent uh capabilities for academic  skills development  at the bottom of our pyramid is  the development um lower cost kind of  opportunities for  um career prep and workforce development  so our staff  that are coming in and working  on the floor as opposed to being in the  research labs  and we're looking at how do we develop  that  broad base of support from an operations  point of view  and there's there's programs that are  focused on that for our industry for  bioprocessing  delaware as a program at the university  of delaware  the delaware biotechnology institute wpi  is a program our north carolina state  has a program jefferson has a program  and they're working on making sure that  our workforce  that's already in the corporate  environment  is is getting prepared and is continuing  to be developed  there's a place in the middle  that we work on at thinking about  how do we collaborate industry and  with academia to develop talent  that comes out as a little bit more  prepared than undergraduates  but isn't quite yet in the workforce and  isn't part of these other workforce  development activities  and that's an area that we really focus  on at kgi  so the uh we partner with nimble and  bpog as  inputs these are consortiums in the  industry uh  nimble is a us-based government  supported  part of the department of commerce uh  it's  established to generate opportunities  for technology and workforce development  in biomanufacturing and bpog is  bio forums operations group is based out  of  england and is a worldwide global  organization focused on  mostly technology platforms but tackling  the need for  developing capabilities across the  industry  so where does that lead us to the future  of work  so we need leaders who understand the  business value of data analytics and are  going to drive the implementation of  these digital solutions  and we need then management to work  across the supply chain  for efficient solutions and support new  work models  that attract and retrain and retain the  key talent  and then we need that talent to  continuously build new skills  so bringing all of that together with  our educational opportunities  and the idea of how do we develop an  employee we have developed this map  of how to build an industry  collaborative  opportunity for talent development  so we start with rigorous course  offerings  focused in depth and as well on emerging  topics as well as  the 50 year old understanding of  heat and mass transfer that are  necessary in our industry  industry participation we want  our industry partners to be part of our  lectures to be  providing guest lectures to do on-site  training  to bring us current tools we also work  for  hands-on experience not only in our labs  which of course are part of our course  offerings but  we want our students to do internships  and to do corporate sponsored projects  so they understand what it's like to  work  in your environments when they leave so  that we meet that need of  when a student comes out of our program  they're ready to contribute to the day  they walk out of the program  and we hope that this drives us to a  talent pool  that at the entry level is incredibly  strong  that allows the or they understand the  business they're getting in so the  retention is high  their innovative employees who are able  to see the global impact of their work  and in conclusion i'm just revisiting um  the pharma 4.0 is really changing the  requirements for the talent pool in our  industry  and we have to address that both through  ongoing programs  for existing employees as well as  through an  understanding and investment in the  academic opportunities that we  offer to students we need to  sustain a strong talent pool with  multi-disciplinary training  and then we strongly recommend that we  work together  across academics and industry to bring  these opportunities to support the  growth of our industry into the future  and i appreciate your attention i  understand that  questions will be forwarded through  email so i appreciate your  questions and look forward to more  collaboration into the future  thank you
 

